<resources>
    <!--
      The app's English strings, and its **fallback resource set**: Android loads this folder for any phone
      whose language has no folder of its own, so a French or Polish tutor reads the app in English rather
      than meeting a missing-resource crash. `AppLanguage.FALLBACK` says the same thing in Kotlin, and the
      two have to agree or the words and the dates would come from different languages.

      `values-ru/` is the Russian set and the app's original. The **reasoning** behind a given wording — why
      «В плане» is short, why undo is «Вернуть» and not «Отменить» — lives there, next to the words it is
      about; what is repeated here is only what a reader of *this* file needs. Every name in one file exists
      in the other: a name that does not is a crash on one of the two languages, which is what
      `StringCoverageTest` is for.

      **Plurals have two forms here and four there.** English selects `one`/`other`; Russian selects
      `one`/`few`/`many`/`other`, and the rule set comes from the *configuration's* locale rather than from
      the folder the string was loaded from — see `Localization.kt`, which is the whole reason this app pins
      a locale at all.
    -->
